-# Layout of a material data manager
+# Layouts of a material data manager
 
 The arrays of a material data manager can be stored component by
-component:
+component. The `layout` member of the initializer, of type
+`MaterialDataManagerLayout`, gives one layout per array. A `FieldLayout`
+converts to it, and then sets the layout of all the arrays:
 
 ~~~~{.cxx}
 auto i = MaterialDataManagerInitializer{};
@@ -101,39 +103,96 @@ i.layout = FieldLayout::COMPONENT_BY_COMPONENT;  // POINT_BY_POINT by default
 auto m = MaterialDataManager(b, n, i);
 ~~~~
 
-The layout is stored in the `layout` member of the material data manager
-and of its material state managers. Everything given point by point to the
-manager follows this layout:
+The layout of each array can also be chosen, for instance to store the
+tangent operator blocks component by component and the states point by
+point:
 
-- the arrays of the states, the tangent operator blocks and the speed of
-  sound;
-- the external state variables with several components given by a
-  `std::span` (the material properties are scalars);
+~~~~{.cxx}
+auto l = MaterialDataManagerLayout{FieldLayout::POINT_BY_POINT};
+l.tangent_operator = FieldLayout::COMPONENT_BY_COMPONENT;
+i.layout = l;
+~~~~
+
+The members of `MaterialDataManagerLayout` are the layouts of:
+
+- the gradients, the thermodynamic forces and the internal state
+  variables of both states (`gradients`, `thermodynamic_forces`,
+  `internal_state_variables`);
+- the values of the external state variables with several components
+  given by a `std::span` (`external_state_variables`);
+- the tangent operator blocks (`tangent_operator`).
+
+The layouts are stored in the `layout` member of the material data
+manager. Its material state managers have a `MaterialStateManagerLayout`,
+with the same members except the tangent operator (see `getStateLayout`).
+The scalar arrays (energies, speed of sound, mass density, material
+properties) have no layout. `getUniformLayout` returns the layout shared
+by all the arrays, if any.
+
+The values given at each point by a `std::span` are in the layout of the
+array they relate to:
+
+- the outputs of `extractInternalStateVariable`: the internal state
+  variables;
+- the outputs of the conversions of the finite strain stresses and
+  tangent operators: the thermodynamic forces and the tangent operator
+  blocks;
 - the inputs of the initialize functions and the outputs of the
-  post-processings given by a `std::span`;
-- the outputs of `extractInternalStateVariable` and of the conversions of
-  the finite strain stresses and tangent operators given by a `std::span`.
+  post-processings relate to no array: they are in the layout of the
+  manager, which must then be uniform. Otherwise, they must be given by a
+  `FieldView`, whose strides are explicit.
 
