+
+## Padding
+
+The `padding` member of the layouts adds unused values to the leading
+dimension of the arrays of the gradients, of the thermodynamic forces, of
+the internal state variables and of the tangent operator blocks: the
+stride between two points becomes `nc + padding` in the `POINT_BY_POINT`
+layout, and the stride between two components becomes `n + padding` in
+the `COMPONENT_BY_COMPONENT` layout. The unused values of the arrays
+allocated by the manager are quiet NaN, and the arrays given by an
+initializer must have the padded size (`getFieldArraySize`).
+
+The padding is a testing facility: a code computing the position of a
+value by hand (`i * nc + c` or `c * n + i`) instead of using the views
+reads a NaN and fails loudly. The `*_stride` members of the material state
+managers and the `K_stride` member of the material data managers remain
+the number of values of a point, not the stride between two points.
+
+## Functions and bindings
+
+`MaterialFunctionManager` takes an initializer and is built on the views
+of the arrays. The variables whose components are contiguous at each
+point (scalar variables, variables of arrays stored point by point, with
+or without padding) are returned by its usual accessors. With the `COMPONENT_BY_COMPONENT` layout,
+`getStridedCoalescedGradient<N>`, `getStridedCoalescedThermodynamicForce<N>`
+and `getStridedCoalescedInternalStateVariable<N>` return strided coalesced
+views of `mgis::function`, which require arrays without padding.
+
+The layouts are exposed by the `C`, `Fortran` and `Python` bindings:
+
+- `C`: `mgis_bv_material_data_manager_initializer_set_layout` sets the
+  layout of all the arrays,
+  `mgis_bv_material_data_manager_initializer_set_array_layout` the layout
+  of one array (`mgis_bv_MaterialArray`), and
+  `mgis_bv_material_data_manager_initializer_set_padding` the padding. The
+  `get_layout` functions report an error if the layouts of the arrays
+  differ: `get_array_layout` gives the layout of one array;
+- `Fortran`: the same functions. The accessors returning multidimensional
+  arrays (gradients, thermodynamic forces, internal state variables,
+  tangent operator) refuse the arrays stored component by component or
+  padded;
+- `Python`: the `MaterialDataManagerLayout` and `MaterialStateManagerLayout`
+  classes. The arrays are `numpy` arrays with the strides of their layouts,
+  padding included.
 
